00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,258
Can you change x

2
00:00:01,258 --> 00:00:02,455
without touching x?

3
00:00:02,675 --> 00:00:03,922
This is Go Part

4
00:00:03,922 --> 00:00:05,586
23, pointers basics.

5
00:00:05,806 --> 00:00:07,119
Watch this little trick.

6
00:00:07,839 --> 00:00:09,262
Ampersand x gives the

7
00:00:09,262 --> 00:00:10,142
address of x.

8
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:11,574
So p points at the

9
00:00:11,574 --> 00:00:12,853
same box in memory.

10
00:00:13,073 --> 00:00:14,296
Star p means the

11
00:00:14,296 --> 00:00:15,977
value inside that box.

12
00:00:16,697 --> 00:00:18,439
Printing star p gives five.

13
00:00:18,659 --> 00:00:20,037
Then we set star p

14
00:00:20,037 --> 00:00:21,339
to ten, and now x

15
00:00:21,339 --> 00:00:22,181
is ten too.

16
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:23,852
Same box, two names.

17
00:00:24,572 --> 00:00:26,017
Pass the address into a

18
00:00:26,017 --> 00:00:27,901
function, and the function can

19
00:00:27,901 --> 00:00:29,534
change your real variable.

20
00:00:29,754 --> 00:00:31,291
Double makes x twenty.

21
00:00:32,011 --> 00:00:34,139
Quick question: which symbol gets

22
00:00:34,139 --> 00:00:35,236
the address of x?

23
00:00:35,456 --> 00:00:36,784
Ampersand or star?

24
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:38,383
